2.1.333:
You have restored me to life.
PAMPHILUS
Now, if you can do any thing, either you yourself, or Byrrhia here, manage, fabricate, invent, contrive some means, whereby she may be given to you;

3.4.584:
What, I do that?
SIMO
I fancied so;

3.5.607:
(Enter PAMPHILUS in haste from SIMO’s house.) PAMPHILUS
Where is he? The villain, who this day—I’m ruined;
and I confess that this has justly befallen me, for being such a dolt, so devoid of sense; that I should have in-trusted my fortunes to a frivolous slave!

4.5.807:
I wish she had.
CRITO
What, not yet? With no favorable omen did I set out for this place; for, upon my faith, if I had known that, I never would have moved a foot hither. She was always said to be, and was looked upon as her sister;

5.3.882:
Miserable that I am!
SIMO
Ha! have you at last found that out only just now, Pamphilus? Long since did that expression, long since, when you made up your mind, that what you desired must be effected by you at any price;

2.3.295:
slovenly, and dirty with filthiness.
CLITIPHO
If this is true, Clinia, as I believe it is, who is there more fortunate than you? Do you mark this girl whom he speaks of, as dirty and drabbish? This, too, is a strong indication that the mistress is out of harm’s way, when her confidant is in such ill plight;

2.3.339:
Find out something of that description, I beseech you.
SYRUS
By all means;

3.1.469:
What shall I do?
CHREMES
Any thing, rather than what you are thinking of;

3.2.553:
The necessity will not arise, I trust.
SYRUS
I’ faith, and I trust so too: nor do I say so now, because I have suspected him in any way;

4.5.774:
What, her that has just been discovered?
SYRUS
The same;

4.8.844:
How much he is mistaken!
MENEDEMUS
Chremes, you are the very person I wanted;

4.8.864:
I’ll say so—what then?
CHREMES
That I will do every thing;

5.1.874:
(Enter MENEDEMUS from his house.) MENEDEMUS
(to himself.)
I am quite aware that I am not so overwise, or so very quick-sighted;

5.5.1049:
Father, I entreat you to forgive me.
MENEDEMUS
Forgive him, Chremes;

2.3.310:
To the point, you simpleton.
CHAEREA
Upon my faith, this is the fact. Now, then, let your promises be made good, if you please, or if indeed the affair is a deserving one for you to exert your energies upon. The girl isn’t like our girls, whom their mothers are anxious to have with shoulders kept down, and chests well girthed, that they may be slender. If one is a little inclined to plumpness, they declare that she’s training for a boxer, and stint her food;

3.2.503:
I’ll do so.
THAIS
Well, what else was I intending to say? O, do you take particular care of that young woman;

3.4.538:
(Enter ANTIPHO.) ANTIPHO
(to himself.)
Yesterday some young fellows of us agreed together at the Piraeus that we were to go shares today in a club-entertainment. We gave Chaerea charge of this matter;

5.8.1067:
Let’s hear.
GNATHO
Do you step a little that way, Thraso. (THRASO stands aside.) In the first place, I wish you both implicitly to believe me in this, that whatever I do in this matter, I do it entirely for my own sake;

1.2.69:
To one who had so much property, that he had more than he could use?
GETA
Do have done;

1.3.156:
What’s the matter?
ANTIPHO
Do you ask the question? You, who have been my confederate in so bold an adventure? How I do wish it had never entered the mind of Phormio to persuade me to this, or to urge me in the heat of my passion to this step, which is the source of my misfortunes. Then I should not have obtained her; in that case I might have been uneasy for some few days;

1.4.179:
(Enter GETA, running, at the other side of the stage.) GETA
(to himself.)
Geta, you are undone, unless you instantly find out some expedient;

1.4.204:
I am not myself.
GETA
But just now it is especially necessary you should be so, Antipho;

2.2.330:
How is that?
PHORMIO
Because the net is never spread for the hawk or the kite, that do us the mischief; it is spread for those that do us none: because in the last there is profit, while with the others it is labor lost. For persons, out of whom any thing can be got, there’s risk from others; they know that I’ve got nothing. You will say: They will take you, when sentenced, into their house;

2.3.384:
Come now, didn’t you know your own cousin-ger-man?
DEMIPHO
You torture me to death;

3.2.528:
Do you try to trifle with him (pointing to PHAEDRIA) in this manner?
DORIO
Why really, on the contrary, Antipho, it’s he trifling with me, for he knew me to be a person of this sort; I supposed him to be quite a different man;

4.2.591:
(Enter GETA, on the other side of the stage, not seeing DEMIPHO or CHREMES.) GETA
(to himself.)
I never saw a more cunning fellow than this Phormio. I came to the fellow to tell him that money was needed, and by what means it might be procured. Hardly had I said one half, when he understood me;

5.6.877:
On my faith, I too have heard the same story.
GETA
Aye, and I’ll give you still further reason for believing it: your uncle in the mean time came out from there; not long after he returned again, with your father;

1.2.76:
(Enter PARMENO from the house of LACHES.) PARMENO
(at the door, speaking to SCIRTUS within.)
If the old man should be asking for me, do you say that I have just gone to the harbor to inquire about the arrival of Pamphilus. Do you hear what I say, Scirtus? If he asks for me, then you are to say so; if he does not, why, say nothing at all;

3.4.439:
But I don’t know the man’s appearance.
PAMPHILUS
Then I’ll tell you how to know it;

4.4.623:
(Enter PHIDIPPUS, from his house.) PHIDIPPUS
(speaking at the door to PHILUMENA, within.)
Upon my faith, I am angry with you too, Philumena, extremely so, for, on my word, you have acted badly;

4.4.639:
The child! What child?
PHIDIPPUS
We have had a grandson born to us;

4.4.692:
What, I?
LACHES
Your own self, and you act unjustly therein. You feign false grounds for discord, that you may live with her when you have got rid of this witness of your actions;

5.3.799:
(Enter PARMENO, moving along with difficulty.) PARMENO
(to himself.)
Upon my faith, my master does assuredly think my labor of little value;

3.3.355:
(Enter DEMEA.) DEMEA
(to himself.)
Utterly undone! I hear that Ctesipho was with Aeschinus at the carrying off of this girl. This sorrow still remains for unhappy me, should Aeschinus be able to seduce him, even him, who promises so fair, to a course of debauchery. Where am I to inquire for him? I doubt he has been carried off to some bad house;

3.4.460:
I’ll accost him. (Approaches HEGIO.) Hegio, I bid you welcome right heartily;
HEGIO
(starting.)

3.4.488:
No doubt of it, Hegio.
HEGIO
Ah! she is now imploring your protection, Demea;

4.2.578:
But that lane is not a thoroughfare.
SYRUS
I’ faith, that’s true; dear, dear, would you take me to be in my senses? I made a mistake. Return to the portico;

4.3.604:
Nay, I’ll go as well.
HEGIO
You act with kindness;

4.7.753:
Having hold of the rope, you will be dancing with them.
MICIO
Like enough;

5.8.948:
What now? What remains to be done?
DEMEA
Hegio here is their nearest relation;
